---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ProjectCard from '../components/ProjectCard.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About | Erik Poppleton" description="About Erik Poppleton - scientist and developer">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)] mb-8">About Me</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
      

      <h2>Background</h2>
      <p>
        Originally from the dry mountains and valleys of Utah, I've since lived the academic life, moving on to a new city for each new research direction.
        I've wandered through many scientific fields, from biophysical characterization of artificial collagen-like molecules during my bachelors at Tufts University, 
        to digging holes in the high-altitude sun for a soil biogeochemistry internship at Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, 
        to a PhD which mixed experiments, simulations, and software infrastructure for the Molecular Programming field at ASU,
        to my current position as a postdoc using simulations to understand RNA interactions at Heidelberg University and the MPI for Polymer Research.
        Each new position has been a new opportunity to explore and grow as a scientist and as a person.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things have driven my scientific journey: a deep love of the natural world and an insatiable curiosity about complex systems.
        I started both my bachelors and PhDs convinced I was going to study life-across-scales, first in wildlife ecology, and then in bioremediation of human-impacted soils.
        Clearly from my resume, I didn't keep that focus, and kept coming back to a question at the other end of the length-scale: how can we design materials from by controlling molecules themselves?
        Nevertheless, when not at the lab, I can often be found wandering the nearest mountains, pausing to take photos of mushrooms and enjoying the susurrations of the leaves.
        Check out the <a href="/research">Research</a> page to learn more about my current research.
      </p>

